    "composition_parti": {
      "group": "massing-and-roof",
      "binding": "specified",
      "variants": [
        {
          "id": "additive-vernacular",
          "status": "forbidden",
          "note": "A revival-era house is composed by a trained architect working from a historical reference (photograph, measured drawing, or pattern-book plate), not grown by accretion the way a real vernacular building was. Pueblo Revival is the family's one deliberate exception: it explicitly revives the additive logic of the Puebloan and Hispano-New Mexican vernacular it quotes, and states so at its own style level, which — because a style's own specified binding always wins over the family's — overrides this prohibition correctly without needing to restate it here."
        }
      ],
      "rule": "Across the family, composition is designed, not accreted — the single exception, Pueblo Revival, overrides this at its own style level. Which of strict-bilateral, near-symmetrical, balanced-asymmetrical or picturesque-asymmetrical composition actually governs is a per-style decision the family does not adjudicate: the 21 members span the full range from Beaux-Arts's axial strict symmetry to Storybook Style's deliberately exaggerated irregularity, with no single value shared by anywhere near a majority of them.",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "note": "This is the one genuinely defensible family-wide fact found in this family's 21 members. Symmetry class itself splits roughly six ways (strict-bilateral, near-symmetrical, balanced-asymmetrical, picturesque-asymmetrical, plus Pueblo Revival's additive-vernacular) with no majority value, so no canonical composition_parti value is asserted — only the one thing essentially the whole family agrees on, which is that it is composed rather than accreted. Every other slot in this kit is left open on the same honest basis: this is the largest and most heterogeneous family in the corpus (12 direct style children plus 9 variants, spanning Beaux-Arts monumental classicism, Pueblo Revival's earth-toned vernacular quotation, and Storybook Style's theatrical cottage), and forcing a shared value anywhere else in the ontology would mean copying one member's specifics and calling it shared, which is the failure mode this work package exists to avoid.",
      "status": "drafted"
    },
